Before deploying encryption, first determine if encryption is an appropriate solution for the specific ICS application, because authentication and integrity are generally the key security issues for ICS applications. Other cryptographic solutions such as cryptographic hashes should also be considered.The use of encryption within an ICS environment could introduce communications latency due to the additional time and computing resources required to encrypt, decrypt, and authenticate each message. For ICS, any latency induced from the use of encryption, or any other security technique, must not degrade the operational performance of the end device or system. Encryption at OSI Layer 2 should be considered, rather than at Layer 3 to reduce encryption latency.In addition, encrypted messages are often larger than unencrypted messages due to one or more of the following:a. Additional checksums to reduce errorsb. Protocols to control the cryptographyc. Padding (for block ciphers)d. Authentication procedurese. Other required cryptographic processes.Cryptography also introduces key management issues. Sound security policies require periodic key changes. This process becomes more difficult as the geographic size of the ICS increases, with extensive SCADA systems being the most severe example. Because site visits to change keys can be costly and slow, it is useful to be able to change keys remotely.If cryptography is selected, the most effective safeguard is to use a complete cryptographic system approved by the NIST/ Communications Security Establishment (CSE) Cryptographic Module Validation Program (CMVP) (http://csrc.nist.gov/cryptval/cmvp.htm). Within this program standards are maintained to ensure that cryptographic systems were studied carefully for weaknesses by a wide range of experts, rather than being developed by a few engineers in a single organization. At a minimum, certification makes it probable that:a. Some method (such as counter mode) will be used to ensure that the same message does not generate the same value each timeb. ICS messages are protected against replay and forgingc. Key management is secure throughout the life cycle of the keyd. The system is using an effective random number generatore. The entire system has been implemented securely.Even then, the technology is only effective if it is an integral part of an effectively enforced information security policy. American Gas Association (AGA) report 12-1 [5] contains an example of such a security policy. While it is directed toward a natural gas SCADA system, many of its policy recommendations could apply to any ICS.The encryption hardware should be protected from physical tampering and uncontrolled electronic connections. Assuming cryptography is the appropriate solution, organizations should select cryptographic protection with remote key management if the units being protected are so numerous or geographically dispersed that changing keys is difficult or expensive. [32]', 'The security controls that fall within the NIST SP 800-53 System and Communications Protection (SC) family provide policy and procedures for protecting systems and data communications components.Supplemental guidance for the SC controls can be found in the following documents:a. NIST SP 800-28 provides guidance on active content and mobile code [67].b. NIST SP 800-52 provides guidance on Transport Layer Security (TLS) Implementations [68]c. NIST SP 800-56 provides guidance on cryptographic key establishment [69].d. NIST SP 800-57 provides guidance on cryptographic key management [70].e. NIST SP 800-58 provides guidance on security considerations for VoIP technologies [71].f. NIST SP 800-63 provides guidance on remote electronic authentication [51].g. NIST SP 800-77 provides guidance on IPsec VPNs [72].', 'Technical Controls', 'Technical Controls', 5, NULL, 'SET.20220414.113656', NULL, NULL, 5723, 13, NULL, 12277)
Supplemental guidance for the SC controls can be found in the following documents:a. NIST SP 800-28 provides guidance on active content and mobile code [67].b. NIST SP 800-52 provides guidance on Transport Layer Security (TLS) Implementations [68]c. NIST SP 800-56 provides guidance on cryptographic key establishment [69].d. NIST SP 800-57 provides guidance on cryptographic key management [70].e. NIST SP 800-58 provides guidance on security considerations for VoIP technologies [71].f. NIST SP 800-63 provides guidance on remote electronic authentication [51].g. NIST SP 800-77 provides guidance on IPsec VPNs [72].', 'Technical Controls', 'Technical Controls', 5, NULL, 'SET.20220414.113656', NULL, NULL, 5724, 13, NULL, 12278)
